---
/**
 * Percentage Calculator — 호주 영어판 첫 도구. 한국어판 /tools/percent/ 와 계산(lib/percent.ts)·스크립트를 같이 쓰고
 * (문구는 scripts/percent.ts 의 EN 사전 — 영어 판 공용), 문장은 호주 자동완성으로 새로 썼다(번역 아님). 산술이라 나라 제도·rates 와 상관없다(조사표 호주 칸 '같음').
 *
 * 소제목은 호주 구글 자동완성에서 왔다(keywords/au-percent-2026-10-20.csv, hl en · gl au):
 *   percentage of a number formula·without calculator·trick / percentage increase·decrease between two numbers·formula /
 *   percentage decrease from 80 to 60 / percentage off price calculator australia / percentage calculator of marks·grade /
 *   percentage increase formula excel
 * GST 같은 호주 세율 숫자는 쓰지 않는다 — rates 로 검증한 값이 아니고 이 도구의 계산이 아니다.
 * title·description 은 같은 영어 판(en-us 등)과 갈리게 Australia 를 넣었다.
 */
import BaseHead from "../../../../components/BaseHead.astro";
import AdSlot from "../../../../components/AdSlot.astro";
import ClarityHead from "../../../../components/ClarityHead.astro";
import GtagHead from "../../../../components/GtagHead.astro";
import TrackLinks from "../../../../components/TrackLinks.astro";
import AdSenseHead from "../../../../components/AdSenseHead.astro";
import SiteHeader from "../../../../components/SiteHeader.astro";
import SiteFooter from "../../../../components/SiteFooter.astro";
import "../../../../styles/global.css";

const title = "Percentage Calculator Australia — Percentage Increase, Decrease & Percentage Off";
const description =
  "Work out a percentage of a number, the percentage increase or decrease between two numbers, and a price after a percentage off. Formulas, marks and Excel.";
---

<html lang="en-AU">
  <head>
    <BaseHead title={title} description={description} />
    <ClarityHead />
    <GtagHead />
    <AdSenseHead />
  </head>
  <body class="bg-texture">
    <SiteHeader />
    <main>
      <h1>Percentage Calculator</h1>
      <p>
        Choose what you're working out, then type in two numbers. Answers are rounded to two decimal places.
      </p>

      <div class="calc-card" data-two>
        <div class="calc-input-wrap">
          <label for="mode">What are you working out?</label>
          <select id="mode" data-seg>
            <option value="ratio" selected>B is what % of A?</option>
            <option value="of">What is B% of A?</option>
            <option value="change">% increase or decrease from A to B</option>
            <option value="adjust">A plus or minus B%</option>
            <option value="original">Original value before a B% change</option>
          </select>
        </div>

        <div class="field" id="dir-row" hidden>
          <label for="dir">Increase or decrease</label>
          <select id="dir" data-seg>
            <option value="down" selected>Decrease (percentage off)</option>
            <option value="up">Increase (mark-up)</option>
          </select>
        </div>

        <div class="calc-input-wrap">
          <label id="a-label" for="a">Whole value A</label>
          <input type="text" inputmode="decimal" id="a" value="200" autocomplete="off" />
        </div>

        <div class="calc-input-wrap">
          <label id="b-label" for="b">Part value B</label>
          <input type="text" inputmode="decimal" id="b" value="50" autocomplete="off" />
          <span class="unit" id="b-unit" hidden>%</span>
        </div>

        <div class="result-hero" aria-live="polite" aria-atomic="true" data-dock="hero-value">
          <p class="label" id="hero-label">B is this percent of A</p>
          <p class="big" id="hero-value">25%</p>
          <p class="hint" id="formula">50 ÷ 200 × 100</p>
        </div>

        <div class="stat-row" data-receipt id="receipt">
          <div class="stat-chip">
            <span class="label" id="l1">Whole value A</span>
            <span class="value" id="v1">200</span>
          </div>
          <div class="stat-chip">
            <span class="label" id="l2">Part value B</span>
            <span class="value" id="v2">50</span>
          </div>
        </div>

        <div class="actions">
          <button id="btn-download" type="button">Save as image</button>
          <button id="btn-share" type="button" class="secondary">Share</button>
        </div>
      </div>

      <h2>Percentage of a number: the formula</h2>
      <p>
        To find B% of A, multiply A by B and divide by 100. 15% of 80 is 80 × 15 ÷ 100 = 12. To go the other way and
        find what percentage one number is of another, divide the part by the whole and multiply by 100:
        12 ÷ 80 × 100 = 15%.
      </p>

      <h2>Working out a percentage without a calculator</h2>
      <p>Break the percentage into easy pieces and add them up:</p>
      <ul>
        <li><strong>10%</strong> — move the decimal point one place left. 10% of 340 is 34.</li>
        <li><strong>5%</strong> — half of 10%. 5% of 340 is 17.</li>
        <li><strong>1%</strong> — move the decimal point two places left. 1% of 340 is 3.4.</li>
        <li><strong>15%</strong> = 10% + 5% = 34 + 17 = 51. <strong>12%</strong> = 10% + 1% + 1% = 34 + 3.4 + 3.4 = 40.8.</li>
      </ul>
      <p>
        Another trick: x% of y is the same as y% of x. 8% of 25 is hard in your head, but 25% of 8 is a quarter of 8, which is 2.
      </p>

      <h2>Percentage increase and decrease between two numbers</h2>
      <p>
        Take the old number away from the new one, divide by the old number and multiply by 100:
        (new − old) ÷ old × 100. A positive answer is an increase, a negative one is a decrease.
      </p>
      <ul>
        <li><strong>Percentage decrease from 80 to 60</strong> — (60 − 80) ÷ 80 × 100 = −25%, a 25% decrease.</li>
        <li><strong>Percentage increase from 60 to 80</strong> — (80 − 60) ÷ 60 × 100 = +33.33%.</li>
      </ul>
      <p>
        The same gap of 20 is a 25% drop one way and a 33.33% rise the other, because you always divide by the number
        you started from.
      </p>

      <h2>Percentage off a price</h2>
      <p>
        The sale price is the original price × (1 − the discount). 30% off $50 is 50 × 0.7 = $35, so you save $15.
        Typing <kbd>50</kbd> <kbd>×</kbd> <kbd>0.7</kbd> on your phone is quicker than working out the discount first.
      </p>
      <p>
        Discounts don't stack by adding. 20% off and then a further 10% off is 0.8 × 0.9 = 0.72 of the price — 28% off,
        not 30%. Run "A plus or minus B%" twice to check.
      </p>

      <h2>Percentage of marks</h2>
      <p>
        Divide the marks you got by the total marks and multiply by 100. 42 out of 60 is 42 ÷ 60 × 100 = 70%. Use
        "B is what % of A?" with the total as A and your mark as B. If a test has sections worth different amounts,
        add up the marks first and divide once — averaging the section percentages gives a different answer.
      </p>

      <h2>Percentage formulas in Excel</h2>
      <p>With the old value in A1 and the new value in B1:</p>
      <ul>
        <li>What percentage B1 is of A1: <code>=B1/A1</code>, then format the cell as a percentage.</li>
        <li>Percentage increase or decrease: <code>=(B1-A1)/A1</code>, formatted as a percentage.</li>
        <li>B1% of A1, with B1 typed as a plain number like 15: <code>=A1*B1/100</code>.</li>
        <li>Price after B1% off: <code>=A1*(1-B1/100)</code>.</li>
      </ul>
      <p>
        If a cell is already formatted as a percentage, 15% is stored as 0.15, so leave out the <code>/100</code>.
        The same formulas work in Google Sheets.
      </p>

      <details>
        <summary>Why doesn't a 20% increase and then a 20% decrease get me back to the start?</summary>
        <p>
          The second step starts from a bigger number. 100 up 20% is 120, and 120 down 20% is 120 × 0.8 = 96.
          To get from 120 back to 100 you need a 16.67% decrease. Check it with "Original value".
        </p>
      </details>

      <details>
        <summary>What's the difference between percentage change and percentage difference?</summary>
        <p>
          Percentage change has a before and an after, so it divides by the old number. Percentage difference compares
          two numbers with no direction and divides by their average: |A − B| ÷ ((A + B) ÷ 2) × 100. For 60 and 80 that's
          20 ÷ 70 × 100 = 28.57%. This calculator does percentage change.
        </p>
      </details>

      <details>
        <summary>What is a percentage point?</summary>
        <p>
          If an interest rate goes from 4% to 5%, it has risen by <strong>1 percentage point</strong>, which is a
          (5 − 4) ÷ 4 × 100 = <strong>25%</strong> increase. Subtracting two percentages gives points; dividing that by
          the old rate gives the percentage change.
        </p>
      </details>

      <details>
        <summary>Can I type a dollar sign or commas?</summary>
        <p>
          Commas and the % sign are fine — 1,200 and 15% both work. Leave out the $ sign and type the number only.
        </p>
      </details>

      <AdSlot label="percent-result-en-au" />
    </main>
    <SiteFooter />
    <script src="../../../../scripts/percent.ts"></script>
    <TrackLinks />
    <script src="../../../../scripts/calc-ui.ts"></script>
  </body>
</html>
